{"step_index":442,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T12:57:21Z","content":"Here is a detailed breakdown of what this command does and how it operates under the hood:\n\n### 1. Breakdown of Each Argument\n\n| Argument | What It Tells Certbot |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `certonly` | **Obtain or renew the certificate only.** Certbot will download and save the certificate files to `/etc/letsencrypt/` without automatically modifying any Nginx configuration files. |\n| `--cert-name extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` | **Targets the existing certificate lineage.** Prevents Certbot from creating a separate new certificate directory (such as `...-0001`). All files will stay in [`/etc/letsencrypt/live/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/`](file:///etc/letsencrypt/live/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/). |\n| `--expand` | **Replaces the existing certificate with an expanded domain list.** Suppresses interactive prompts asking whether you want to replace or branch the certificate. |\n| `-d extranetclient...,map.extranetclient...` | **Specifies the complete list of domains.** Let's Encrypt certificates cover all specified domains as **Subject Alternative Names (SAN)**. You must provide *both* the original domain and the new subdomain so both remain valid. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. What Happens Under the Hood (Step-by-Step)\n\n```\n1. Reads Renewal Config (/etc/letsencrypt/renewal/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf)\n   └── Identifies authenticator (e.g. webroot, nginx plugin, or dns-01 RFC2136)\n\n2. Contacts Let's Encrypt ACME Server\n   └── Sends request for BOTH:\n       - extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n       - map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n\n3. ACME Challenge Validation\n   └── Proves control over map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\n\n4. Certificate Generation & Symlink Updates\n   ├── Archives new certificate files to:\n   │   /etc/letsencrypt/archive/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/certX.pem\n   └── Updates existing symlinks in:\n       /etc/letsencrypt/live/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/fullchain.pem\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Why This Is Important for Your Setup\n\n1. **Zero Nginx Config Changes Required**:\n   Because `--cert-name` keeps the exact same folder path ([`/etc/letsencrypt/live/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/`](file:///etc/letsencrypt/live/extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/)), your existing Nginx directives in [`02-extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf`](file:///etc/nginx/sites-available/02-extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf) don't need any path edits.\n2. **Eliminates the PHP cURL / UI Failure**:\n   The new certificate will contain:\n   * `SAN: DNS:extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`\n   * `SAN: DNS:map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`\n   \n   When Centreon Web's backend connects to `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`, PHP cURL verifies the hostname against the SAN list, succeeds, and the **\"Test connection to server\"** button turns green.\n3. **Activation**:\n   A simple `sudo systemctl reload nginx` immediately loads the updated certificate into memory with zero downtime.","thinking":"**Deconstructing Certbot Certificate Acquisition**\n\nThe command's `certonly` flag directs certificate acquisition/renewal exclusively. This means the process will store the generated certificate and keys. It will not attempt to configure any webserver settings automatically. The certificate will reside within a specified directory.\n\n\n**Analyzing Certbot's Certificate Expansion**\n\nThe `--cert-name` flag ensures the existing certificate is updated rather than a new one being created, preserving current symbolic links. The `--expand` argument enables adding domains to an existing certificate without generating a new one. The `-d` parameter specifies both the original and new domains to be included in the SAN extension. The ACME challenge is performed to validate domain ownership. If the validation is successful, Let's Encrypt will issue an X.509 certificate.\n\n\n"}
