{"step_index":456,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:02:20Z","content":"**No, running it locally on your Debian Trixie workstation will fail**, for two fundamental reasons:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why Running It Locally Will Not Work\n\n1. **Traffic Doesn't Reach Your Machine (ACME HTTP-01)**:\n   * Public DNS maps `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` to `109.62.64.18`.\n   * The datacenter firewall forwards port 80/443/9443 to the reverse proxy **`th2-revproxyp-fe1` (`172.30.198.33`)**, not to your local workstation.\n   * When Let's Encrypt queries `http://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/.well-known/acme-challenge/<TOKEN>`, the request hits the reverse proxy. If Certbot is running on your local Debian Trixie, it will never receive the ACME challenge and will fail with `Connection refused` or `404 Not Found`.\n\n2. **The Certificate Must Be on the Reverse Proxy**:\n   * Nginx terminates TLS on **`th2-revproxyp-fe1`**. Even if you bypassed validation, the certificate files must physically reside in `/etc/letsencrypt/live/...` on `172.30.198.33`.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Where and How to Run It\n\nThis must be executed **directly on the reverse proxy `th2-revproxyp-fe1` (`172.30.198.33`)**. \n\nAs documented in your infrastructure procedures, the reverse proxy uses the **webroot authenticator** via `/var/www/_letsencrypt`.\n\n#### Step 1: Connect to the reverse proxy\n```bash\nssh -p 30 spui-runner@172.30.198.33\n# or your admin account\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Test with `--dry-run` first\n```bash\nsudo certbot certonly --webroot -w /var/www/_letsencrypt \\\n  --cert-name map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr \\\n  -d map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr \\\n  --agree-tos --dry-run\n```\n\n#### Step 3: Run for real\n```bash\nsudo certbot certonly --webroot -w /var/www/_letsencrypt \\\n  --cert-name map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr \\\n  -d map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr \\\n  --agree-tos\n```\n\nOnce obtained, the certificate will be in place at `/etc/letsencrypt/live/map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr/` ready for your dedicated Nginx configuration.","thinking":"**Investigating Certificate Issuance Method**\n\nThe provided command line, located at line 49, reveals how the certificate for `extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` was issued. The issuance occurred on the server `th2-revproxyp-fe1` using the webroot method with the specified directory. It's crucial to understand how certificate issuance functions.\n\nLet's test this locally; the webroot path on `th2-revproxyp-fe1` is `/var/www/_letsencrypt`, and testing on Debian Trixie necessitates adapting this command line with `map.extranetclient.outremer-tel